{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/service at exact clean pushed head 088dde2c197068d3848cc66894cb049cffe70cde, branch request/resident-browser-security-headers. Fulfils request 6f62c6bd under promise 2deabcd3, and completes the original request f26cf90c. This replaces artifact c123146d, which was correct about the routes and wrong about the resident.\n\nWHAT THE PREVIOUS HEAD GOT WRONG. It installed the policy on Server.Handler and claimed no route could omit it. Codex's ratified changes-requested 4c60a2ca found the hole: the real composition wraps TrustedHostHandler OUTSIDE Server.Handler, and that wrapper refuses a non-loopback mutation Host by writing its own response and returning without delegating. A reachable API error therefore carried none of the four protections, and the architecture sentence claiming every response carries them was false. My tests could not have seen it: they drove Server.Handler alone, which is the composition that does not exist in production.\n\nA SECOND ONE, FOUND BY DOING WHAT THE REVIEW ASKED. Codex asked me to check the still-outer residentHTTPHandler paths against the same claim rather than assuming. One is the same shape: cmd/gs/main.go answers 500 when the /v0/status write deadline cannot be cleared, and returns without delegating. That is a second reachable response that carried nothing, and nobody had named it. telemetryRuntime.Handler is clean -- it either returns next untouched or always calls it.\n\nHOW BOTH ARE CLOSED. One exported SetBrowserProtections over the same map is now the only way to obtain the values, and each refusal that writes a whole response calls it. No values are duplicated and no handler keeps a copy; what repeats is a call, at exactly the two places that originate a response without delegating.\n\nI did not move the policy outward to the composition root, which would have been the obvious fix. internal/perfscenario composes server.Handler() directly at four sites, so relocating the policy would have covered the resident and quietly left that consumer bare. Keeping Server.Handler self-protecting and having the two outer refusals opt in is the shape that leaves no composition uncovered.\n\nEVIDENCE. The new regression drives the real TrustedHostHandler(server.Handler()) composition with Host example.invalid:7777, asserts the 400 host refusal by body as well as status, then checks all four headers. Removing the setter from that refusal fails it: dropped Referrer-Policy = \"\", want \"no-referrer\". The three earlier mutations still bite: removing the routes wrapper, weakening frame-ancestors from 'none' to 'self', and deleting nosniff from the map.\n\nGATES at the exact head, all run: gofmt clean; go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./internal/service 17.722s and ./internal/wireparity 0.762s; go test -count=1 ./cmd/gs 121.363s, which matters because the second fix is in that package; go test -count=1 ./... with no failing package; make docs ok 55.747s; git diff --check clean. Worktree clean, based on current main a7a9abe7, pushed.","body":{"branch":"request/resident-browser-security-headers","commit":"088dde2c197068d3848cc66894cb049cffe70cde","path":"internal/service","tests":"gofmt; go vet ./...; go test -count=1 ./internal/service ./internal/wireparity; go test -count=1 ./cmd/gs; go test -count=1 ./...; make docs; git diff --check"}}